RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE

Office of City Clerk

May 28, 2020

The Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne convened in a special meeting on this date
beginning at 6:00 p.m. via WebEx Electronic Conference Meeting for the purpose of collective
labor bargaining with the Cheyenne Professional Fire Fighters, International Association of Fire
Fighters, Local No. 279.  Present were:  MAYOR – Marian J. Orr; COUNCIL MEMBERS – Mr.
Case, Mr. Cook, Mr. Esquibel, Mr. Laybourn, Mr. Luna, Dr. Rinne, Mr. Roybal, Mr. Shanor and
Mr. White. Also present: Kylie Soden, Deputy City Clerk; Michael O’Donnell, City Attorney;
Alessandra McCoy Fakelman, Deputy City Attorney; Robin Lockman, City Treasurer and Chief
Greg Hoggatt, Fire and Rescue Department.

Pursuant to Wyoming State Statute §16-4-405(a)(x) of the Public Meetings Act allows,
Mayor Orr advised the governing body would be going into executive session to consider or tender
offers regarding salaries, wages or other terms and conditions of employment during negotiations
with the fire fighters union and that any motion approved by the governing body to move into
executive session during the special meetings would be pursuant to this section of statute.  Mayor
Orr further explained when the governing body met in executive session, they would be meeting
in a private electronic conference, and that Fire Union representatives will meet privately off-line
for the purpose of meeting to caucus.

Pursuant to Wyoming State Statute §16-4-405(a)(x), Mr. Roybal moved to meet in
executive session, seconded by Mr. Luna.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the
governing body.  Mayor Orr identified, in addition to members of the governing body present, the
Deputy City Clerk, City Attorney, Deputy City Attorney, City Treasurer and Chief of the Fire and
Rescue Department would also be present at the executive session.  [City Clerk note: Audio
recording of the special meeting was stopped; executive session convened at 6:04 p.m. via
electronic conference meeting]

Following closure of the executive session, Dr. Rinne reconvened the special meeting at
7:25 p.m. [City Clerk note: Mayor Orr left during executive session due to a prior commitment].
City Attorney Michael O’Donnell reviewed the “crib notes” which had also been provided to
members of the governing body and the Union.  He explained the crib notes contain City and
Union offers that are being presented for the current contract and the 2020-2021 contract pursuant
to Section 7, Re-Negotiations.  Mr. O’Donnell noted the proposals mainly involve next fiscal
year’s contract and budget, but both contracts must be reviewed under Section 7 to accomplish
this.  David Evans, Attorney representing the Union, didn’t express any opposition with the process
of presenting and reviewing the offer.  Mr. O’Donnell reviewed and explained information
presented during previous discussion with the Union in conjunction with Section 7, Re-
Negotiations and Section 45, Labor Management Relation.  He advised the City proposed an
overall budget cut of 8% resulting in $880,000 in savings to include foregoing longevity pay
resulting in $143,600 in savings and reducing 3 firefighter trainee positions resulting in $266,727
```

### Page 2 of 4

```text
in savings.  Union response and City counteroffers included: 1 year cut of Uniforms ($49,750
suggested savings) – City counteroffered to accept the proposal, but offered that it only be ½ of
uniform costs and add in $250 from administration uniform costs ($25,125 savings); Eliminate
Administrative Professional Development (suggested savings of $1,000) – City rejected the
proposal; Eliminate Prevention Professional Development (suggested savings of $3,000) – City
rejected the proposal; Freeze Office Manager Position (suggested savings of $95,903) – City
rejected the proposal; Utilize EOD vs. the pay for holidays (est. savings of $130,000 based upon
Union calculation of 50% of employees electing EOD vs. pay) – it was noted the suggested savings
calculation is in error.  According to the Treasurer’s office and based upon most recent data, the
short term savings would be approximately $62,000.  In the long term, the proposal will likely cost
the City more than the current approach -the City rejected the proposal; Promotion of Lieutanant
to Division Chief and cut Lt. of Prevention position (estimated savings of $131,525) – it was noted
the estimated savings is not as much as the Union prediction as the specific lieutenant is already
acting in the capacity of Division Chief.  If the Lt. of Prevention position is cut and the acting Lt.
of Prevention returns to shift operations, the savings is only estimated at $21,400.  The proposed
budget is based upon the loss of three firefighter positions and the budget already contains a
reduction of $75,500 with a reorganization of day staff moving back to shift operations to support
the line crews.  The City rejected the proposal and counteroffered the following – keep the 3
firefighter trainees employed and wait for 3 expected retirements in the next 6 months.  The retiring
positions will not be re-filled in the upcoming fiscal year therefore the number of firefighters will
drop from 91 to 88 and remain there until the end of the fiscal year absent any kind of change of
circumstances like federal funding (savings of $133,364).  The City re-offered elimination of
longevity pay for 1 year (savings of $143,600) and offered the following: The Fire Chief will
obtain $30,000 in savings by making internal changes, but the changes aren’t part of the Collective
Labor Agreement negotiations and the balance of needed savings will be made up from reserves
in the amount of $78,238.  Mr. Evans expressed concern with dropping the number of firefighters
from 91 to 88, requested clarification on the proposed amount of savings and inquired if a SAFER
grant was applied for.  Mr. O’Donnell and Fire and Rescue Chief Greg Hoggatt explained the grant
wasn’t applied for since it was for new firefighter positions, not existing ones.  Mr. O’Donnell
advised the City will continue work with the Union to obtain CARES act grants and any other
stimulus bill grants that may be available. Comment was made that the amendments should contain
language related to the necessity of the amendments are due to the effects of Covid-19.  Mr. Evans
requested a recess at 7:44 p.m. for the purpose of reviewing the City’s offer.

Upon reconvening at 7:58 p.m., Mr. Evans explained the Union’s priority is maintaining
91 firefighters.  He noted the Union thought they offered $400,000 in budget cuts, but
acknowledged the City Treasurer’s recalculated amount.  He provided the following information
related to Union counteroffers – accept Fire Chief’s $30,000 in savings by making internal
changes, re-offer forgoing uniforms for 1 year, re-offered utilizing EOD vs. pay for holidays and
in an effort to maintain 91 firefighter positions, the Union offered taking a 1% cut in pay.  Mr.
Case moved to meet in executive session, seconded by Mr. Luna.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes”
– all members of the governing body present. [City Clerk note: Audio recording of the special
meeting was stopped; executive session convened at 8:06 p.m. via electronic conference meeting]

Following closure of the executive session Dr. Rinne reconvened the special meeting at
8:28 p.m.  Mr. O’Donnell reviewed the following counteroffers from the City – accept Union’s
```

### Page 3 of 4

```text
offer of uniform reductions, acknowledge Fire Chief’s proposed $30,000 in administrative savings
and reoffer reduction of longevity pay for one year. The $126,400 balance will become the pay
reduction for firefighters for next year.  The City Treasurer will have an accurate percentage
calculation for the pay reduction by tomorrow. The total savings will be $350,000.  Mr. Evans
commented on the following: longevity pay being a strong position for the Union, $208,000 (1%
proposed cut) in proposed savings is for the base firefighter wage, significance of offering 1% pay
reduction and the purpose of collective bargaining process. Upon inquiry, Mr. O’Donnell
confirmed the City had not accepted the EOD vs. pay for holiday proposal. Mr. Evans further
commented on proposed “philosophical” cuts and expressed the importance of considering all
proposals during the current budget crisis. Mr. Evans requested a recess at 8:30 p.m.

Upon reconvening at 8:42 p.m., Mr. Evans noted he believes the interest of the governing
body is meeting a budget crisis opposed to resolving annual contractual concerns.  He advised they
believe the EOD vs. pay for holidays predicted savings of $62,000 is a minimum amount and will
probably be more.  He reiterated the Union’s commitment to resolving budget concerns based on
their proposed 1% pay cut.  Related to the uncertainty of the EOD dollar figure, Mr. O’Donnell
inquired if the Union would entertain moving the EOD savings of $62,000 to the pay reductions.
Mr. Evans advised they feel the proposed 1% pay cut is the most they can do with membership
given they previously agreed to no raises.  He further noted comp time has been used for years in
lieu of overtime on holidays.  Mr. Cook moved to meet in executive session, seconded by Mr.
White.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present. [City Clerk
note: Audio recording of the special meeting was stopped; executive session convened at 8:51 p.m.
via electronic conference meeting]

Following closure of the executive session, Dr. Rinne reconvened the special meeting at
9:02 p.m.  Mr. O’Donnell advised the City will accept the Union’s EOD vs. pay for holidays offer
on 2 conditions: 1) that it is only for the FY 2021 contract and has no influence on future contracts
and 2) the number of firefighters that can have a day off is reduced from 4 to 3 to manage the
numbers of those taking leave.  That number can be reviewed daily after sick call. Dr. Rinne
clarified that longevity pay will remain in place.  Mr. Evans requested a recess at 9:04 p.m.

Upon reconvening at 9:17 p.m., Mr. Evans commented on comp time management
concerns.  Chief Hoggatt explained the preschedule aspect noting miscellaneous scheduled leaves
contributes to availability.  By reducing the number of firefighters who can take a day off from 4
to 3, overtime will be able to be managed better. If a firefighter has pre-scheduled a day off, they
will be allowed to take the time off.  Mr. Evans requested a recess at 9:22 p.m.

Upon reconvening at 9:26 p.m., Union representative TJ Haws explained there are
members who have already scheduled future vacation time and there would be the potential they
will not be able to take their scheduled time with reducing the allowed number of firefighters off
daily from 4 to 3, therefore, the Union declines the proposal of decreasing the number of
firefighters allowed to take a day off from 4 to 3.  Mr. Shanor moved to meet in executive session,
seconded by Mr. Case.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” – all members of the governing body present.
[City Clerk note: Audio recording of the special meeting was stopped; executive session convened
at 9:30 p.m. via electronic conference meeting]
```

### Page 4 of 4

```text
Following closure of the executive, session Dr. Rinne reconvened the special meeting at
9:38 p.m.  Mr. O’Donnell explained the proposal is if a 4th spot is already scheduled for time off,
that person will be allowed to take the time, but if that spot isn’t already filled, it won’t be.  This
is only for FY 2021 until the Covid-19 crisis is over.  Mr. Evans requested a recess at 9:39 p.m.

Upon reconvening at 9:46 p.m., Mr. Evans requested freezing the 4th slot through
December 31, 2020, scheduling vacations after January 1, 2021, and then freezing any remaining
available 4th slots through June 30, 2021.  Upon inquiry on when freezing of the 4th time off slot
would start, Chief Hoggatt explained there are protected pre-retirements and FMLA and
deployment leaves that will be contributing to overtime through the end of June.  The requirement
needs to be in effect for the contractual time limit due to Covid-19 issues.  Mr. Evans
acknowledged the need to finalize offers, the need for forecasting overtime needs when retirements
occur and that prescheduled vacations will be honored and in the future will be accommodated if
it doesn’t cause overtime.  He made suggestions regarding hiring and academy time frames.  Mr.
Evans accepted reducing the number of firefighters who can take a day off from 4 to 3.  Chief
Hoggatt confirmed freezing of the 4th slot will begin July 1, 2020 and will only be frozen if it is
still open.  Mr. O’Donnell noted the Attorney’s office will craft the final contract language.

Dr. Rinne stated the purpose of the special meeting had been met, and there being no
business previously noted for the special meeting, adjourned the meeting at 9:59 p.m.

Submitted by,

Kylie Soden,

Deputy City Clerk

This notice is available in alternative, accessible formats upon request.

Published:
Wyoming Tribune-Eagle

June 11, 2020
```
